{:edb/db-before-t 1961 :edb/db-after-t 1962 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535274 50 #inst "2026-10-08T21:00:55.366Z" 13194139535274 true] [17592186147429 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341124 13194139535274 true] [17592186147431 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341138 13194139535274 true] [17592186147432 1020 #edb/ref 17592186341131 13194139535274 true] [17592186148163 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535274 true] [17592186148163 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/d3a06bdc7a63a86651752ff70331010920d24728e7f4e4ad9f067eb860ebaf4b.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-1$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-1)^{2}(y+1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-1)^{2}$" 13194139535274 true] [17592186148163 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96513.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n1. $\\frac{dy}{dx} = y^{2} - 1$\n2. $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (y - 1)^{2}(y + 1)$\n3. $\\frac{dy}{dx} = (y - 1)^{2}$" 13194139535274 false] [17592186148163 1145 "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=1.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,1).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-1.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)={y}^{2}-1,$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-1,1),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation I has the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}(y+1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-1)^{2}(y+1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,1.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y-1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=1$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation III does not have the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535274 true] [17592186148166 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535274 true] [17592186148166 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/b958bd4db4353cfe7a852b79f7144d0b48b85c48b3d97f813b09db2858ec110a.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" 13194139535274 true] [17592186148166 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2532/Source/Images/q-96516.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{dy}{dx} = f(y)$, where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" 13194139535274 false] [17592186148166 1145 "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-3,y=0$ and $y=3.$ Therefore $y=-3,0,3$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-3,0)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (0,3).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/666ae97e41e825161c68cc6146a06c70b87c6d84b11ae8116c0d415c340987fa.png)" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341124 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341125 13194139535274 true] [17592186341124 1089 "q-97915" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341124 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535274 true] [17592186341124 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/25d8d89ac4fdb3937596606d3ac347e08e3492c3dca65c3886c6eca8e3e121b5.png)\n\nConsider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where the graph of $f(y)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the phase line corresponding to this differential equation?" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341124 1113 #uuid "8f487ce6-961a-5d12-8ceb-0da56b2d670b" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341124 1145 "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-1,y=0$ and $y=2.$ Therefore $y=-1,0,2$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-1,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (0,2).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/2b51f096104fe981a341096c945881595529d17061d0e5656e1ef3d1a9f79df4.png)" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341126 13194139535274 true] [17592186341125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341127 13194139535274 true] [17592186341125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341128 13194139535274 true] [17592186341125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341129 13194139535274 true] [17592186341125 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341130 13194139535274 true] [17592186341125 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535274 true] [17592186341125 1041 "selection" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341125 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341129 13194139535274 true] [17592186341125 1110 #uuid "165d44d5-4225-5c4d-b377-bfddc521721c" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341126 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/73994f96f68cd43ee0377ea2005479e0c927e37dc0a15e2e9da35c1c8900afa0.png" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341126 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535274 true] [17592186341126 1111 #uuid "77a44e6b-d2f8-5484-9cde-1ed8a7547b5b" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341127 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/78373cbb2e323dcbfbc40770fb47a59939670ca8e27a70b3ef19eaa04ba3f939.png" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341127 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535274 true] [17592186341127 1111 #uuid "ac0bb362-7aa5-567b-863a-70f246ba8470" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341128 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/2b5796afbbcf6ec35a0980a82957ddbf74813b0113b621a0b77cfc24f8706838.png" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341128 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535274 true] [17592186341128 1111 #uuid "9dd8ec8d-cbfa-51d1-b0aa-9aff0445243a" 131941395