 arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/fcc9f998111493167284cf5844645ac2201a230b073a9336604fb790ba16bad4.png)" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341133 13194139535274 true] [17592186341132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341134 13194139535274 true] [17592186341132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341135 13194139535274 true] [17592186341132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341136 13194139535274 true] [17592186341132 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341137 13194139535274 true] [17592186341132 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535274 true] [17592186341132 1041 "selection" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341132 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341137 13194139535274 true] [17592186341132 1110 #uuid "13325c0c-2256-5a30-ba60-d90a721de45c" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341133 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/d46036d0f5c0fc689d1123e3512a446b94688f6fa94df017ee26682860ed6b64.png" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341133 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535274 true] [17592186341133 1111 #uuid "093b88c7-ea2c-58fd-a8b2-bde1e7dee6cd" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341134 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/03d9436c922b602b3e3388dfc21b6e8c20aae96f70b378de706b3c96b7ded253.png" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341134 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535274 true] [17592186341134 1111 #uuid "3b41e1bd-5d3a-5001-90ed-633a34435d1f" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341135 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/86bcece5228ba30310e4b4ed663c5efcb551609474fa924034efac08aa055a23.png" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341135 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535274 true] [17592186341135 1111 #uuid "ba82f68b-7f08-5823-8363-0993b3caaf7e" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341136 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/f95aa207248151fc7035fcd6dcdea8de58ebff368aa886b18413249bf30225ba.png" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341136 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535274 true] [17592186341136 1111 #uuid "7d5386a3-bea2-5aaf-acca-f6f751e65352" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341137 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/f22dc056accfeae38eea6fb0207adfe4676bd8bd875cbeb96c078f4eb6457a6e.png" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341137 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535274 true] [17592186341137 1111 #uuid "1d62cd27-e989-5d8e-84ab-cc994623c357" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341138 1017 #edb/ref 17592186341139 13194139535274 true] [17592186341138 1089 "q-98632" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341138 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535274 true] [17592186341138 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14061054/assets/ca296c3a8a2989fd5e5ca647ad4f7d0f4890aa3caf8d9e0a1fce98498d2d7d64.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y+1)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1)$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-y-2$" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341138 1113 #uuid "b30c866d-fa3d-5692-b249-cd39aee84dde" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341138 1145 "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,2).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=(y+1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=(y+1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solution is $y=-1$ only.\n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}=(y-2)^{2}(y+1).$\n\nFirst, $f(y)=(y-2)^{2}(y+1),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nHowever, we note that $f(y)<0$ for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1).$ This does *not* correspond to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{2}-y-2.$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=(y+1)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for  $y\\in (-1,2),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341140 13194139535274 true] [17592186341139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341141 13194139535274 true] [17592186341139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341142 13194139535274 true] [17592186341139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341143 13194139535274 true] [17592186341139 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341144 13194139535274 true] [17592186341139 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535274 true] [17592186341139 1041 "selection" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341139 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341142 13194139535274 true] [17592186341139 1110 #uuid "a0769a07-0b20-52e2-b52d-b895a1561a9f" 13194139535274 true] [17592186341140 1034 "None of the listed" 13194139535274 true] [175